<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Related work</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On-policy distillation.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> formulations emphasize the exposure-mismatch problem in offline distillation and train the student on its own sampled trajectories with dense teacher feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Recent work shows that this idea has become a practical post-training component for large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Zheng, 2026</a>)</cite>, including large-to-small distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, capability consolidation with multiple teachers or post-training stages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenBMB, 2026</a>)</cite>, and on-policy self-distillation from privileged information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hübotter et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Our work differs from these papers by studying the parameter update induced by <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span>, rather than proposing a new <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> objective or training recipe.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Parameter geometry of post-training.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A second line of related work analyzes the parameter geometry of post-training.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al. (2026a)</a></cite> show that <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span> fine-tunes small subnetworks in large language models, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite> argue that <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span> learns away from the principal directions of source weights.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al. (2025)</a></cite> further study reinforcement-learning-induced parameter dynamics and find that dominant low-rank directions can predict later reasoning improvements.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al. (2026b)</a></cite> connect this sparse update regime to optimizer choice, showing that SGD can be surprisingly competitive for <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span>.
We ask whether these properties persist when the sparse reward signal is replaced by dense teacher supervision, which places <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> between conventional dense-supervised fine-tuning and sparse-reward on-policy training.
Our AdamW-versus-SGD result shows that the static sparsity analogy does not automatically carry over to optimizer choice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptation and optimization methods.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the spectral and off-principal results relate to parameter-efficient adaptation and optimizer design.
Low-rank adaptation methods such as <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LoRA</span> exploit the concentration of fine-tuning updates in a low-dimensional additive subspace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman and Lab, 2025</a>)</cite>.
Orthogonal finetuning methods such as <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OFT</span>/<span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BOFT</span> instead parameterize adaptation through orthogonal transformations, aiming to preserve hyperspherical energy and pretrained representation structure while changing the model’s function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Muon is relevant from the optimizer side because it treats hidden-layer weights as matrices and orthogonalizes the momentum update before applying it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Recent work argues that this spectral normalization can fail in VLA and <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span> post-training and proposes high-pass remedies, making these variants a more plausible starting point for <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> than vanilla Muon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
These methods motivate future <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span>-native optimization strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Post-training objectives for LLMs</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the training objectives that define the regimes compared in this paper.
Let <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denote a prompt, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> a response, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> the trainable policy, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> a teacher policy, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> a reference policy used for KL regularization when applicable.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SFT and sequence-level KD.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supervised fine-tuning (<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SFT</span>) optimizes the negative log-likelihood of fixed target responses:</p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SFT}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{\star})\sim\mathcal{D}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}^{\star}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}^{\star})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SFT}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{\star})\sim\mathcal{D}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}^{\star}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}^{\star}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Sequence-level knowledge distillation (<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SeqKD</span>) uses the same maximum-likelihood objective, but the target response <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a teacher rather than drawn from human annotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim and Rush, 2016</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SeqKD}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y^{T}\sim\pi_{T}(\cdot|x)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}^{T}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}^{T})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>SeqKD</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SeqKD}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y^{T}\sim\pi_{T}(\cdot|x)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}^{T}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}^{T}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Both objectives are dense in the sense that every target token supplies a supervised learning signal, but the trajectories are offline-collected and fixed before the student update.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RLVR and GRPO.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span>) samples responses from the current or recent policy and optimizes a scalar reward <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, often with a KL penalty to keep the policy close to <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{RLVR}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\left[r(x,y)-\beta\sum_{t=1}^{T}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\right)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>RLVR</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{RLVR}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\left[r(x,y)-\beta\sum_{t=1}^{T}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">GRPO is a commonly used <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span> optimizer that removes the learned critic by sampling a group
of responses
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> for the same prompt and normalizing their rewards within the group
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
With
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i}=(r_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G}))/(\mathrm{std}(\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G})+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i}=(r_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G}))/(\mathrm{std}(\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G})+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}|x,y_{i,&lt;t})/\pi_{\mathrm{old}}(y_{i,t}|x,y_{i,&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}|x,y_{i,&lt;t})/\pi_{\mathrm{old}}(y_{i,t}|x,y_{i,&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math>,
a clipped GRPO objective can be written as</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{J}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|y_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|y_{i}|}\left[g_{i,t}(\theta)-\beta k_{i,t}(\theta)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|y_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|y_{i}|}\left[g_{i,t}(\theta)-\beta k_{i,t}(\theta)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min\!\Bigl(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i},\,\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{i}\Bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min\!\Bigl(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i},\,\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\right)\hat{A}_{i}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E7Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x,y_{i,&lt;t})\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot|x,y_{i,&lt;t})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x,y_{i,&lt;t})\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot|x,y_{i,&lt;t})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">RLVR therefore uses on-policy or near-on-policy samples but usually receives sparse sequence-level
reward feedback.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OPD.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> combines on-policy student rollouts with dense teacher feedback.
One style, which we call GKD-style <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span>, applies a token-level teacher-student divergence on prefixes generated by the student <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}\text{-}\mathrm{GKD}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}D\!\left(\pi_{T}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\,\|\,\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>OPD</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>GKD</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mpadded style="width:0.686em;" width="0.686em"><mi>D</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}\text{-}\mathrm{GKD}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}D\!\left(\pi_{T}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\,\|\,\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x,y_{&lt;t})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is typically a <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence token distribution loss.
Another style, which we call PG-style <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span>, treats distillation as policy optimization on student-generated samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
An abstract form is the reverse-KL sequence objective</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}\text{-}\mathrm{PG}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{T}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>OPD</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>PG</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}\text{-}\mathrm{PG}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})-\log\pi_{T}(y_{t}|x,y_{&lt;t})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which can be optimized with policy-gradient estimators because the sampled trajectory depends on the student policy.
This PG view is connected to reverse-KL distillation through a simple score-function identity.
For a fixed prefix <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=(x,y_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=(x,y_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math>, define the sampled-token estimator
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}(a;c)=\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)-\log\pi_{T}(a|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}(a;c)=\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)-\log\pi_{T}(a|c)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The full-vocabulary reverse KL at this prefix is</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\pi_{T}(\cdot|c)\right)=\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)k_{1}(a;c)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\pi_{T}(\cdot|c)\right)=\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)k_{1}(a;c).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Its gradient is</p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\pi_{T}(\cdot|c)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\pi_{T}(\cdot|c)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{\theta}\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)k_{1}(a;c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{\theta}\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)k_{1}(a;c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{a}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\right]k_{1}(a;c)+\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\nabla_{\theta}k_{1}(a;c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{a}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\right]k_{1}(a;c)+\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\nabla_{\theta}k_{1}(a;c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)}\left[k_{1}(a;c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\right]+\sum_{a}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)}\left[k_{1}(a;c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\right]+\sum_{a}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E11Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)}\left[k_{1}(a;c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|c)}\left[k_{1}(a;c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Here we use
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)=\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)=\pi_{\theta}(a|c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}k_{1}(a;c)=\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}k_{1}(a;c)=\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)</annotation></semantics></math>, since the
prefix <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and the teacher <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed.
The final equality uses <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{a}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{a}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)=\nabla_{\theta}1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{a}\nabla_{\theta}\pi_{\theta}(a|c)=\nabla_{\theta}1=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, if <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is detached and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-k_{1}(a;c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-k_{1}(a;c)</annotation></semantics></math> is used as the policy-gradient
advantage, the score-function loss
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}(a;c)=\operatorname{sg}[k_{1}(a;c)]\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>PG</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}(a;c)=\operatorname{sg}[k_{1}(a;c)]\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)</annotation></semantics></math>
has gradient
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}(a;c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}(a;c)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|c)</annotation></semantics></math>, giving an unbiased single-sample
estimator of the reverse-KL gradient up to practical modifications such as clipping,
off-policy importance ratios, and advantage normalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> styles differ from <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SeqKD</span> by training on student-generated trajectories, and differ from <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RLVR</span> by replacing sparse scalar rewards with dense teacher-derived feedback.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Model pairs and training details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ A.3 Model pairs and training details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dense Supervision, Sparse Updates:On the Sparsity and Geometry of On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> lists the checkpoint pairs used in the delta analysis.
These pairs cover LLMs with PG-style <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> updates and VLMs with GKD-style <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> updates, and they span the three use cases of OPD discussed in Section <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Related work ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dense Supervision, Sparse Updates:On the Sparsity and Geometry of On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For training and evaluation, we use the public implementations and original configurations from <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">verl</span>/HybridFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> codebase, and the <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPSD</span> codebase where applicable.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/verl-project/verl" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/verl-project/verl</a>, <a href="https://github.com/thunlp/OPD" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/thunlp/OPD</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/siyan-zhao/OPSD" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/siyan-zhao/OPSD</a>.</span></span></span>
The DS-Qwen <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> runs use DAPO-Math-17K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, the VLM <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GRPO</span> and <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> runs use Geo3K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, the Qwen3-1.7B <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> pair is a public checkpoint distilled from Qwen3-4B-Base-GRPO, and the <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPSD</span> run uses subsets of OpenThoughts_math_30k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guha et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Tables <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.3 Model pairs and training details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dense Supervision, Sparse Updates:On the Sparsity and Geometry of On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.3 Model pairs and training details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dense Supervision, Sparse Updates:On the Sparsity and Geometry of On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> summarize the core training configurations for the controlled DS-Qwen and Qwen2.5-VL <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span> runs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Core training configuration for DS-Qwen <span id="A1.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPD</span>.</figcaption>
<div id="A1.T5.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:113.4pt;vertical-align:-54.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-34.8pt,9.9pt) scale(0.85111854807333,0.85111854807333) ;">
<table id="A1.T5.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T5.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T5.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Setting</th>
<td id="A1.T5.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Value</td></tr>
